\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Equilibrium With Complete Markets and Asset Pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.1.
 Time 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 versus sequential trading
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This chapter describes competitive equilibria for a pure exchange infinite
 horizon economy with stochastic endowments.
 This economy is useful for studying risk sharing, asset pricing, and consumptio
n.
 We describe two market structures: an 
\emph on
Arrow-Debreu
\emph default
 structure with complete markets in dated contingent claims all traded at
 time 0, and a sequential-trading structure with complete one-period 
\emph on
Arrow securities
\emph default
.
 These two entail different assets and timings of trades, but have identical
 consumption allocations.
 Both are referred to as complete markets economies.
 They allow more comprehensive sharing of risks than do the incomplete markets
 economies, or the economies with imperfect enforcement or imperfect information
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.2.
 The physical setting: preferences and endowments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In each period 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

, there is a realization of a stochastic event 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Let the history of events up and until time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 be denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}=\left[s_{0},s_{1},\ldots,s_{t}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 The unconditional probability of obsering a particular sequence of events
 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 is given by a probability measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 We write conditional probabilities as 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t}|s^{\tau})$
\end_inset

, which is the probability of observing 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 conditional upon the realization of 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\tau}$
\end_inset

.
 In this chapter, we shall assume that trading occurs after observing 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

, which we capture by setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{0}(s_{0})=1$
\end_inset

 for the initially give value of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In section 8.9, we shall follow much of the literatures in macroeconomics
 and econometrics and assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is induced by a Markov process.
 We wait to impose that special assumption because some important findings
 do not require making that assumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 agents named 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 owns a stochastic endowment of one good 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 that depends on the history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 is publicly observable.
 Household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 purchases a history-dependent consumption plan 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}=\left\{ c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} _{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 and orders these consumption streams by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(c^{i})={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}\beta^{t}u\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]\pi_{t}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The right side is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}^{i})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the mathematical expectation operator, conditioned on 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Here 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)$
\end_inset

 is an increasing, twice continuously differentiable, strictly concave function
 of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $c\geq0$
\end_inset

 of one good.
 The utility function satisfies the Inada condition
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
One role of this Inada condition is to make the consumption of each agent
 strictly positive in every date-history pair.
 A related role is to deliver a state-by-state borrowing limit to impose
 in economies with sequential trading of Arrow securities.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \lim_{c\rightarrow0}}u^{\prime}(c)=+\infty
\]

\end_inset

A 
\emph on
feasible allocation
\emph default
 satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i}}c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{i}}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.3.
 Alternatie trading arrangements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.4.
 Pareto Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a benchmark against which to measure allocations attaned by a market
 economy, we seek efficient allocations.
 An allocation is a list of sequences of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}=\{c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, one for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, and it is said to be efficient if it is Pareto optimal: it has the property
 that any reallocation that makes one household strictly better off also
 makes one or more other households worse off.
 We can find efficient allocations by posing a Pareto problem for a fictitious
 social planner.
 The planner attaches nonnegative 
\emph on
Pareto weights
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

 on the consumers and choose allocations 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i},\ i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\max & {\displaystyle \sum_{i}\lambda_{i}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\sum_{s^{t}}\beta^{t}u\left(c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right)\pi_{t}(s^{t})\right)}\\
\mbox{subject to} & \sum_{i}c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\leq\sum_{i}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Note that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}$
\end_inset

's are used to map 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}$
\end_inset

 to all possible Pareto efficient allocations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We call an allocation 
\emph on
efficient
\emph default
 if it solves this problem for some set of nonnegative 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}\mbox{'s}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 be a nonnegative Lagrange multiplier on the feasibility constrant for time
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, and form the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}\left\{ \sum_{i}\lambda_{i}\beta^{t}u(c_{t}^{i}(s^{t}))\pi_{t}(s^{t})+\theta_{t}(s^{t})\sum_{i=1}^{I}\left[y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition for maximizing 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{i}(s^{t}))\pi_{t}(s^{t})=\lambda_{i}^{-1}\theta_{t}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i,t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Taking the ratio for consumers 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{i}(s^{t}))}{u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{1}(s^{t}))}=\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\lambda_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, adjusting for the weights, marginal utilities are equalized across
 consumers.
 This equation implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
effic_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=u^{\prime}{}^{-1}\left(\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\lambda_{i}}u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{1}(s^{t}))\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Plugging this equation into the feasibility condition at equality gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i}u^{\prime}{}^{-1}\left(\lambda_{i}^{-1}\lambda_{1}u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{1}(s^{t}))\right)=\sum_{i}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is one equation in 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 The right hand side is the realized aggregate endowment, so the left hand
 side is a funciton only of the aggregate endowment.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 depends only on the current realizaiton of the aggregate endowment and
 neither on the specific history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 leading up to that outcome nor on the realization of individual endowments.
 This implies that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 depends only on the aggregate endowment realization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
An efficient allocation is a function of the realized aggregate endowment
 and depends neither on the specific history leading up to that outcome
 nor on the realizations of individual endowments; 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=c_{\tau}^{i}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}^{\tau}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j}y_{t}^{j}(s^{t})=\sum_{j}y_{t}^{j}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To compute the optimal allocation, first solve (6) for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

, then solve 
\begin_inset Formula $(5)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Note from (5) that only the ratios of the Pareto weights matter, so that
 we are free to normalize the weights, i.e., to impose 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}\lambda_{i}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.4.1.
 Time invariance of Pareto weights
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Through equations (5) and (6), the allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 assigned to consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 depends on a time-invariance way on the aggregate endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j}y_{t}^{j}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's share of the aggregate endowment varies directly with his Pareto weight
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.5.
 Arrow-Debreu time-zero trading
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now describe how an optimal allocation can be attained by a competitive
 equilibrium with the Arrow-Debreu timing.
 Households trade dated history-contingent claims to consumption.
 There is a complete set of securities.
 Trades occur at time 0, after 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

 has been realized.
 At 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

, households can exchange claims on time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption, contingent on history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 at price 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 The superscript 0 refers to the date at which trades occur, while the subscript
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 refers to the date that deliveries are to be made.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The household's problem is to choose 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{c^{i}}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\sum_{s^{t}}\beta^{t}u\left(c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right)\pi_{t}(s^{t})
\]

\end_inset

subject its budget constraint
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\sum_{s^{t}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\leq\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\sum_{s^{t}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is the price of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption contingent on history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 in terms of an abstract unit of account or numeraire.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Underlying the 
\emph on
single 
\emph default
budget constraint (7) is the fact that multilateral trades are possible
 through a clearing operation that keeps track of net claims.
 All trades occur at time 0.
 After time 0, trades that were agreed to at time 0 are executed, but no
 more trades occur.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Each household has a 
\emph on
single
\emph default
 budget constrant (7) to which we attach a Lagrange multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We obtain the first-order conditions for the household's problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta^{t}u^{\prime}[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})]\pi_{t}(s^{t})=\mu_{i}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The left side is the derivative of total utility with respect to the time
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 component of consumption.
 Each household has its own 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 that is independent of time.
 We use the following definition:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A 
\emph on
price system
\emph default
 is a sequence of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

.
 An 
\emph on
allocation
\emph default
 is a list of sequences of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}=\left\{ c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} _{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, one for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Notice that equation (8) implies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{u^{\prime}[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})]}{u^{\prime}[c_{t}^{j}(s^{t})]}=\frac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{j}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, ratios of marginal utilities between pairs of agents are constant
 across all histories and dates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A competitive equilibrium is a feasible allocation and a price system such
 that, given the price system, the allocation solves each household's problem.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An equilibrium allocation solves the feasibility constraint, the budget
 constraint, and equation (9).
 Note that equation (9) implies that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=u^{\prime-1}\left\{ u^{\prime}[c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})]\frac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{1}}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting this into the feasability condition at equaility gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i}u^{\prime-1}\left\{ u^{\prime}[c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})]\frac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{1}}\right\} =\sum_{i}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The right side of equation (11) is the current realization of the aggregate
 endowment.
 It does not per se depend on the specific history leading up to this outcome;
 therefore, the left side, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

, must also depend only on the current aggregate endowment.
 It follows from equation (10) that the equilibrium allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 depends only on the economy's aggreagte endowment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\shape up
\emph on
The competitive equilibrium allocation is a function of the realized aggregate
 endowment and depends neither on the specific history leading up to that
 outcome nor on the realizations of individual endowments; 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=c_{\tau}^{i}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}^{\tau}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{j}y_{t}^{j}(s^{t})=\sum_{j}y_{\tau}^{j}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.5.1.
 Equilibrium pricing function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i},\ i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

 is an equilibrium allocaiton.
 Then the marginal condition (8) gives the price system 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 as a function of the allocation to household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the price system is a stochastic process.
 because the units of the price system are arbitrary, one of the prices
 can be normalized at any positive value.
 We shall set 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}^{0}(s_{0})=1$
\end_inset

, putting the price system in units of time 0 goods.
 This choice implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}=u^{\prime}\left[c_{0}^{i}(s_{0})\right]$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.5.2.
 Optimality of equilibrium allocation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium allocation is a particular Pareto optimal allocation,
 one that sets the Pareto weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{i}=\mu_{i}^{-1}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the unique (up to multiplication by a positive scalar) set of Pareto
 weights associated with the competitive equilibrium.
 Furthermore, at the competitive equilibrium allocation, the 
\emph on
shadow prices
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 for the associated planning problem equal the prices 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 for goods to be delieved at date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 contingent on history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 associated with the Arrow-Debreu competitive equilibrium.
 That the allocations for the planning problem and the competitive equilibrium
 are aligned reflects the two fundamental theorems of welfare economics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.5.3.
 Equilibrium computation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To compute an equilibrium, we have somehow to determine ratios of the Lagrange
 multipliers, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{1}},\ i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

, that appear in equation (10) and (11).
 The following 
\emph on
Negishi algorithm
\emph default
 accomplishes this.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Fix a positive value for one 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

, say 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1}$
\end_inset

, throughout the algorithm.
 Guess some positive values for the remaining 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

's.
 Then solve equations (1) and (11) for a candidate consumption allocation
 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i},\ i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Use (8) for any household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 to solve for the price system 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots I$
\end_inset

, check the budget constraint (7).
 For those 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's for which the const of consumption exceeds the value of their endowment,
 raise 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

, while for those 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's for which the reverse inequality holds, lower 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Iterate to convergence on steps 1-3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Multiplying all of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i}$
\end_inset

's by a positive scalar amounts simply to a change in units of the price
 system.
 That is why we are free to normalize as we have in step 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.5.4.
 Interpretation of trading arrangement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the competitive equilibrium, all trades occur at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 in one market.
 Deliveries occur after 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

, but no more trades.
 A vast clearing or credit system operates at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

.
 It ensures that condition (7) holds for each household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 A symptom of the once-and-for-all trading arrangement is that each household
 faces one budget constraint that accounts for all trades across dates and
 histories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.6.
 Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.6.1.
 Example 1: risk sharing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that the one-period utility function is of the constant relative
 risk-aversion form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(c)=(1-\gamma)^{-1}c^{1-\gamma},\ \gamma>0
\]

\end_inset

 Then equation (9) implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]^{-\gamma}=\left[c_{t}^{j}(s^{t})\right]^{-\gamma}\frac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{j}}
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=c_{t}^{j}(s^{t})\left(\frac{\mu_{i}}{\mu_{j}}\right)^{-\frac{1}{\gamma}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (12) states that time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 elements of consumption allocations to distinct agents are constant fractions
 of one another.
 With a power utility function, it says that individual consumption is perfectly
 correlated with the aggregate endowment or aggregate consumption.
 Also, equation (12) implies that conditional on the history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is independent of the household's individual endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The fractions of the aggregate endowment assigned to each individual are
 independent of the realization of 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, there is extensive cross-history and cross-time consumption smoothing.
 The constant-fractions-of-consumption characterization comes from these
 two aspects of the theory: (1) complete markets and (2) a homothetic one-period
 utility function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.6.2.
 Example 2: No aggregate uncertainty
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let the stochastic event 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 take values on the unit interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 There are two households, with 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{1}(s^{t})=s_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{2}(s^{t})=1-s_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the aggregate endowment is constant, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=1$
\end_inset

.
 Then equation (11) implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{1}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is constant over time and across histories, and equation (11) implies that
 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{2}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is also constant.
 Thus, the equilibrium allocation satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=\bar{c}^{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Then from equation (8)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})=\beta^{t}\pi_{t}(s^{t})\frac{u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})}{\mu_{i}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's budget constraint implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})}{\mu_{i}}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}\beta^{t}}\pi_{t}(s^{t})\left[\bar{c}^{i}-y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using the time 0 good as numeraire, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}^{0}(s_{0})=1$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{u^{\prime}(\bar{c}^{i})}{\mu_{i}}=1
\]

\end_inset

Also,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}\pi_{t}(s^{t})=1}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, solving equation (14) for 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}^{i}$
\end_inset

 gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{c}^{i}=(1-\beta){\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}\beta^{t}\pi_{t}(s^{t})y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Summing equation (15) verifies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}^{1}+\bar{c}^{2}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.6.3.
 Example 3: periodic endowment processes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the special case of the previous example in which 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 is deterministic and alternates between the values 1 and 0; 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 odd, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 even.
 Thus, the endowment processes are perfectly predictable sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,0,1,\ldots)$
\end_inset

 for the first agent and 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,1,0,\ldots)$
\end_inset

 for the second agent.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}^{t}$
\end_inset

 be the history of 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,0,1,\ldots)$
\end_inset

 up to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Evidently, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(\tilde{s}^{t})=1$
\end_inset

, and the probability assigned to all other histories up to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is zero.
 The equilibrium price system is then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})=\begin{cases}
\beta^{t} & \mbox{if}\ s^{t}=\tilde{s}^{t}\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

when using the time 0 good as numeraire, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0}^{0}(\tilde{s}_{0})=1$
\end_inset

.
 From equation (15), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\bar{c}^{1} & = & (1-\beta){\displaystyle \sum_{j=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{2j}=\frac{1}{1+\beta}\\
\bar{c}^{2} & = & (1-\beta)\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{j=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{2j}=\frac{\beta}{1+\beta}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Consumer 1 consumes more every period because he is richer by virtue of
 receiving his endowment earlier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.7 Primer on asset pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Many asset-pricing models assume complete markets and price an asset by
 breaking it into a sequence of history-contingent claims, evaluating each
 component of that sequnce with the relevant 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

state price deflator
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

, then adding up those values.
 The asset is viewed as 
\emph on
redundant
\emph default
, in the sense that it offers a bundle of history-contingent dated claims,
 each component of which has already been priced by the market.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.7.1.
 Pricing redundant assets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ d_{t}(s^{t})\right\} ^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 be a stream of claims on time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption, where 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is a measurable function of 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The price of an asset entitling the owner to this stream must be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{0}^{0}(s_{0})={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})d_{t}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If this equation did not hold, someone could make unbounded profits by synthesiz
ing this asset through purchases or sales of history-contingent dated commoditie
s and then either buying or selling the asset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.7.2.
 Riskless consol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As an example, consider the price of a 
\emph on
riskless consol
\emph default
, that is, an asset offering to pay one unit of consumption for sure each
 period.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{t}(s^{t})=1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, and the price of this asset is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.7.3.
 Riskless strips
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As another example, consider a sequence of 
\emph on
strips 
\emph default
of payoffs on the riskless consol.
 The time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 strip is just the payoff process 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{\tau}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=t\geq0$
\end_inset

, and 0 otherwise.
 Thus, the owner of the strip is etitled only to the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 coupon.
 The value of the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 strip at time 0 is evidently
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})
\]

\end_inset

Compare this to the price of the consol (19).
 Of course, we can think of the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-period riskless strip as simply a 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

-period zero-coupon bond.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.8 Sequential trading: Arrow securities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This section describes an alternative market structure that achieves the
 same equilibrium allocation as the Arrow-Debreu equilibrium.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.8.1 Arrow securities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We build on an insight of Arrow that one-period securities are enough to
 implement complete markets, provided that new one-period markets are reopened
 for traing each period.
 Thus, at each date 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

, trades occur in a set of claims to one-period-ahead-state contingent consumpti
on.
 We describe a competitive equilibrium of this sequential-trading economy.
 With a full array of these one-period-ahead claims, the sequential-trading
 arrangement attains the same allocation as the competitive equilibrium
 we described earlier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.8.2.
 Insight: wealth as an endogenous state variable
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A key step in finding a sequential-trading arrangement is to identify a
 variable to serve as the state in a value funciton for the household at
 date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 We find this state by taking an equilibrium allocation and price system
 for the (Arrow-Debreu) time 0 trading structure and applying a guess-and-verify
 method.
 We begin by asking the following question.
 In the competitive equilibrium where all trading takes place at time 0,
 excluding its endowment, what is the implied wealth of household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 after history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

? In period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, conditional on history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, we sum up the value of the household's purchased claims to current and
 future goods net of its outstanding liabilities.
 Since history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 is realized, we discard all claims and liabilities contingent on another
 initial history.
 For example, household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's net claim to delivery of goods in a future period 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\geq t$
\end_inset

, contingent on history 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}^{\tau}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}^{t}=s^{t}$
\end_inset

, is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[c_{\tau}^{i}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})-y_{\tau}^{i}(\tilde{s}^{\tau})\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the household's wealth, or the value of all its current and future
 net claims, expressed in terms of the date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption good is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})={\displaystyle \sum_{\tau=t}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\tau}|s^{t}}}q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})\left[c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})-y_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})=y_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the feasibility constraint (2) at equality implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{I}}\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=0,\ \forall\ t,s^{t}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In moving from the Arrow-Debreu economy to one with sequential trading,
 we can match up the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 wealth of the household in the sequential economy with the tail wealth
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 from the Arrow-Debreu economy computed in equation (2).
 But first we have to say something about debt limits, a feature that was
 absent in the Arrow-Debreu economy because we imposed (7).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.8.3.
 Debt limits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In moving to the sequential formulation, we shall need to impose some restrictio
ns in this section.
 We'll synthesize restrictions that work by starting from the equilibrium
 allocation of the Arrow-Decreu economy (with time 0 markets), and find
 some state-by-state debt limits that suffice to support sequential trading.
 Often we'll refer to these weakest possible debt limits as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

natural debt limits.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 These limits come from the commonsense requirement that it has to be 
\emph on
feasible
\emph default
 for the consumer to repay his state contingent debt in every possible state.
 Given our assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 must be nonnegative, that feasibility requirement leads to the natural
 debt limits that we now describe.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})$
\end_inset

 be the Arrow-Debreu price, denominated in units of the date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption good.
 Consider the value of the tail of agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's endowment sequence at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 in history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{t}^{i}(s^{t})={\displaystyle \sum_{\tau=t}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\tau}|s^{t}}}q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})y_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We call 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 the 
\emph on
natural debt limit
\emph default
 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 in history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 It is the maximal value that agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 can repay starting from that period, assuming that his consumption is zero
 forever.
 From now on, we shall require that household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

 and history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t-1}$
\end_inset

 cannot promise to pay more than 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 conditional on the realization of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 tomorrow, because it will not be feasible to repay more.
 Note that household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

 faces one such borrowing constraint for each possible realization of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 tomorrow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.8.4.
 Sequential trading
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There is a sequence of markets in one-period-ahead state-contingent claims
 to wealth or consumption.
 At each date 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

, households trade claims to date 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 consumption, whose payment is contingent on the realization of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 denote the claims to 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption, other than its endowment, that household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 brings into time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 in history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})>0$
\end_inset

, then household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 purchased a security, i.e., the houehold saved its money, so it was a lender
 in the security market.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})<0$
\end_inset

, then household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 sold a security, i.e., the household borrowed money, so it was a borrower
 in the security market.
 In each period, total borrowing is equal to total lending, hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A 
\emph on
distribution of wealth
\emph default
 is a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{a_{t}}(s^{t})=\left\{ a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} _{i=1}^{I}$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})$
\end_inset

 gives the price of one unit of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 consumption, contingent on the realizaiton 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, when the hisotry at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that we are guessing that this function exists.
 The household faces a sequence of budget constriants for 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

, where the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 budget constrain is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})+{\displaystyle \sum_{s_{t+1}}}a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})\leq y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})+a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

At time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, a household chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})\right\} $
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})\right\} $
\end_inset

 is a vector of claims on time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 consumption, one element of the vector for each value of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 realizaiton of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 To rule out Ponzi schemes, we impose the state-by-state borrowing constraints
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-a_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\leq A_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is computed in equation (21).
 This condition says restricts consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from borrowing more than his debt limit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{t}^{i}(s^{t}:s_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 be the nonnegative Lagrange multiplier on the budget constraint (22) for
 time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The Lagrangian can then be formed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L^{i}={\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\sum_{s^{t}}\left\{ \beta^{t}u\left(\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right)\pi_{t}(s^{t})+\eta_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\left[y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})+a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-\sum_{s_{t+1}}Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t})a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})\right]+{\displaystyle \sum_{s_{t+1}}}\nu_{t}^{i}(s^{t};s_{t+1})\left[A_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})+a_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

for a given initial wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}^{i}(s_{0})$
\end_inset

.
 The first-order conditions for maximizing 
\begin_inset Formula $L^{i}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})\}_{s_{t+1}}$
\end_inset

 are:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\beta^{t}u^{\prime}(\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t}))\pi_{t}(s^{t}) & = & \eta_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\\
\eta_{t}^{i}(s^{t})Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t}) & = & \eta_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})+\nu_{t}^{i}(s^{t};s_{t+1})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 In the optimal solution to this problem, the natural debt limit (23) will
 not be binding, and hence the Lagrange multipliers 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{t}^{i}(s^{t};s_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 are all equal to zero for the following reason: if there were any history
 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t+1}$
\end_inset

 leading to a binding natrual debt limit, the household would from then
 on have to set consumption equal to zero in order to honor its debt.
 Because the household's utility function satisfies the Inada condition,
 that would mean that all future marginal utilities would be infinite.
 Thus, it is trivial to find alternative affordable allocations that yield
 higher expected utility by postponing earlier consumption to periods after
 such a binding constraint, i.e., alternative preferable allocaitons where
 the natural debt limits no longer bind.
 After setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{t}^{i}(s^{t};s_{t+1})=0$
\end_inset

 in equation (25), the first order conditions imply the following conditions
 on the optimally chosen consumption allocation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t})=\beta\frac{u^{\prime}(\tilde{c}_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1}))}{u^{\prime}(\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t}))}\pi_{t}(s^{t+1}|s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A 
\emph on
sequential-trading competitive equilibrium
\emph default
 is an initial distribution of wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{a_{0}}(s_{0})$
\end_inset

, an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \tilde{c}^{i}\right\} _{i=1}^{I}$
\end_inset

, and pricing kernels 
\begin_inset Formula $\{Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
1.
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, given 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}^{i}(s_{0})$
\end_inset

 and the pricing kernels, the consumption allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{i}$
\end_inset

 solves the household's problem;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
2.
 for all realizaitons of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{t}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

, the households' consumption allocation and implied asset holdings 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t}),\left[a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})\right]_{s+1}\right\} $
\end_inset

 satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i}\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=\sum_{i}y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\ \mbox{and}\ \sum_{i}a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that this definition leaves open wealth.
 The Arrow-Debreu equilibrium with complete markets at time 0 in effect
 pinned down a 
\emph on
particular 
\emph default
distribution of wealth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.8.5.
 Equivalence of allocations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By making an appropriate guess about the form of the pricing kernels, it
 is easy to show that a competitive equilibrium allocaiton of the complete
 markets model with time 0 trading is also a sequential-trading competitive
 equilibrium allocation, one with a particular initial distribution of wealth.
 Thus, take 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 as given from the Arrow-Debreu equilibrium and suppose that the pricing
 kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})$
\end_inset

 makes the following recursion true
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{t+1}^{0}(s^{t+1})=Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})=q_{t+1}^{t}(s^{t+1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t+1}^{t}(s^{t+1})$
\end_inset

 is the price of one unit of consumption delivered at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t+1}$
\end_inset

 in terms of the date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} $
\end_inset

 be a competitive equilibrium allocation in the Arrow-Debreu economy if
 equation (27) is satisfied, that allocaiton is also a sequential-trading
 compeititve equilibrium allocation.
 To show this fact, take the household's first-order conditions (8) for
 the Arrow-Debreu economy from two successive periods and divide one by
 the other to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\beta u^{\prime}\left[c_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\right]\pi(s^{t+1}|s^{t})}{u^{\prime}\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]}=\frac{q_{t+1}^{0}(s^{t+1})}{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})}=Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If the pricing kernel satisfies equation (27), this equation is equivalent
 with the first-order condition (26) for the sequential-trading competitive
 equilibrium economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
If the 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 is a time-zero Arrow-Debreu equilibrium, then we can find prices of one-period-
ahead arrow securities 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t})$
\end_inset

 and asset holdings 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{t}^{i}(s_{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(c,a,Q)$
\end_inset

 become a Sequential equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
The definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 is given by the FOC above:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t})=\frac{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t+1})}{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It remains for us to choose initial wealth of the sequential-trading equilibrium
 so that the sequential-trading competitive equilibrium duplicates the Arrow-Deb
reu competitive equilibrium allocation.
 We conjecture that the initial wealth vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{a_{0}}(s_{0})$
\end_inset

 of the sequential-trading economy should be chosen to be the null vector.
 This a natural conjecture because it means that each household must rely
 on its own endowment stream to finance consumption, in the same way that
 households are constrained to finance their history-contingent purchases
 for the infinite future at time 0 in the Arrow-Debreu economy.
 To prove that the conjecture is correct, we must show that this particular
 initial wealth vector enables household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 to finanace 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} $
\end_inset

 and leaves no room to increase consumption in any period and history.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The proof proceeds by guessing that, at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t\geq0$
\end_inset

 and history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 chooses an asset portfolio given by 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})=\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t+1}$
\end_inset

.
 The value of this asset portfolio expressing in terms of the date 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption good is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
{\displaystyle \sum_{s_{t+1}}}a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t}) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t+1}|s^{t}}}\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})q_{t+1}^{t}(s^{t+1})\nonumber \\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{\tau=t+1}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\tau}|s^{t}}}q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})\left[c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})-y_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where we have invoked expressions (20) and (27).
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We have also used the following identities,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{\tau}^{t+1}(s^{\tau})q_{t+1}^{t}(s^{t+1})=\frac{q_{\tau}^{0}(s^{\tau})}{q_{t+1}^{0}(s^{t+1})}\frac{q_{t+1}^{0}(s^{t+1})}{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})}=q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})\ \mbox{for}\ \tau>t
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

To demonstrate that household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 can afford this portfolio strategy, we now use budget constraint (22) to
 compute the implied consumption plan 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 First, in the initial period 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{0}^{i}(s_{0})=0$
\end_inset

, the substitution of equation (29) into budget constraint (22) at equality
 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{c}_{0}^{i}(s_{0})+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t}}}q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]=y_{t}^{i}(s_{0})+0
\]

\end_inset

This expression together with budget constraint (7) at equality imply 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{c}_{0}^{i}(s_{0})=c_{0}^{i}(s_{0})$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, the proposed asset portfolio is affordable in period 0
 and the associated consumption level is the same as in the competitive
 equilibrium of the Arrow-Debreu economy In all consecutive future period
 
\begin_inset Formula $t>0$
\end_inset

 and histories 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 in constraint (22) by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

, and after noticing that the value of the asset portfolio in (29) can be
 written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
{\displaystyle \sum_{s_{t+1}}}a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1},s^{t})Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t}) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{s^{t+1}|s^{t}}}\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})q_{t+1}^{t}(s^{t+1})\nonumber \\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{\tau=t+1}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\tau}|s^{t}}}q_{\tau}^{t}(s^{\tau})\left[c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})-y_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})\right]+q_{t}^{t}(s^{t})\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]-q_{t}^{t}(s^{t})\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]\\
 & = & \Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})-y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{t}(s^{t})=1$
\end_inset

 is the numeraire.
 It follows immediately from (22) that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{c}_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 for all periods and histories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have shown that the proposed portfolio strategy attains the same consumption
 plan as in the competitive equilibrium of the Arrow-Debreu economy, but
 what precludes household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from further increasing current consumption by reducing some component
 of the asset portfolio? The answer lies in the debt limit restrictions
 to which the household must adhere.
 In particular, if the household wants to ensure that consumption plan 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})\right\} $
\end_inset

 can be attained starting next period in all possbile future states, the
 household should subtract the value of this commitment to future consumption
 from the natural debt limit in (21).
 Thus, the household is facing a state-by-state borrowing constraint that
 is more restrictive than (21): for any 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t+1}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-a_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\leq A_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})-{\displaystyle \sum_{\tau=t+1}^{\infty}}{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\tau}|s^{t+1}}}q_{\tau}^{t+1}(s^{\tau})c_{\tau}^{i}(s^{\tau})=-\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\geq\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})
\]

\end_inset

Hence, household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 does not want to increase consumption at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 by reducing next period's wealth below 
\begin_inset Formula $\Upsilon_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})$
\end_inset

 because that would jeopardize the attainment of the preferred consumption
 plan satisfying the first-order conditions (26) for all future periods
 and histories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\{c_{t}^{i}(s^{t}),a_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t})$
\end_inset

 is a Sequential-trading equilibrium, then we can find prices of history-conting
ent claims such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(c,q)$
\end_inset

 becomes a time-zero Arrow-Debreu equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
8.9.
 Recursive competitive equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have estiablished that the equilibrium allocations are the same in the
 Arrow-Debreu economy with complete markets in dated contingent claims all
 traded at time 0, and in a sequential-trading economy with complete one-period
 Arrow securities.
 This finding holds for arbitrary individual endowment processes 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right\} _{i}$
\end_inset

 that are measurable funcitons of the history of events 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

, which in turn are governed by some arbitrary probability measure 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

.
 At this level of generality, both the pricing kernels 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})$
\end_inset

 and the wealth distributions 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{a_{t}}(s_{t})$
\end_inset

 in the sequential-trading economy depend on history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, these objects are time-varying functions of all past events 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ s_{\tau}\right\} _{\tau=0}^{t}$
\end_inset

, which makes it extremely difficult to formulate an economic model that
 can be used to confront empirial observations.
 What we want is a framework where economic outcomes are functions of a
 limited number of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

state variables
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that summarize the effects of past events and current information.
 This desire leads us to make the following specializaiton of the exogenous
 forcing processes that facilitate a recursive formulation of the sequential-tra
ding equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.9.1.
 Endowments governed by a Markov process
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(s^{\prime}|s)$
\end_inset

 be a Markov chain with given initial distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{0}(s)$
\end_inset

 and state space 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in S$
\end_inset

.
 That is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Prob}(s_{t+1}=s^{\prime}|s_{t}=s)=\pi(s^{\prime}|s)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Prob}(s_{0}=s)=\pi_{0}(s)$
\end_inset

.
 The chain induces a sequence of probability measures 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t})$
\end_inset

 on histories 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 via the recursions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi_{t}(s^{t})=\pi(s_{t}|s_{t-1})\pi(s_{t-1}|s_{t-2})\ldots\pi(s_{1}|s_{0})\pi_{0}(s_{0})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In this chapter we have assumed that trading occurs after 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

 has been observed, which we capture by setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{0}(s_{0})=1$
\end_inset

 for the initially given value of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Because of the Markov property, the conditional probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}(s^{t}|s^{\tau})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t>\tau$
\end_inset

 depends only on the state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{\tau}$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 and does not depend on the history before 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\pi_{t}(s^{t}|s^{\tau})=\pi(s_{t}|s_{t-1})\pi(s_{t-1}|s_{t-2})\ldots\pi(s_{\tau+1}|s_{\tau})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we assume that households' endowments in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 are time invariant measurable funcitons of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=y^{i}(s_{t})$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 this assumption means that each household's endowment follows a Markov
 process, since 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 itself is governed by a Markov process.
 Of course, all of our previous results continue to hold, but the Markov
 assumption imparts further structure to the equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.9.2.
 Equilibrium outcomes inherit the Markov property
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Proposition 2 asserted a particular kind of history independence of the
 equilibrium allocation that prevails under any stochastic process for the
 endowments.
 That is, each individual's consumption is a function only of the current
 realization of the aggregate endowment and does not depend on the specific
 history leading up to that outcome.
 Now, under the asusmption that the endowments are governed by a Markov
 process, it follows immediately from equations (10) and (11) that the equilibri
um allocation is a funciton only of the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})=c^{i}(s_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After substituting (33) and (34) into (26), the pricing kernel in the sequential
-trading equilibrium is then a funciton only of the current state,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{t}(s_{t+1}|s^{t})=\beta\frac{u^{\prime}(c^{i}(s_{t+1}))}{u^{\prime}(c^{i}(s_{t}))}\pi(s_{t+1}|s_{t})\equiv Q(s_{t+1}|s_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, we can also establish history independence of the relative prices in
 the Arrow-Debreu economy:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*
Given that the endowments follow a Markov process, the Arrow-Debreu equilibrium
 price of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{date}-}t\geq0$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{t}$
\end_inset

 consumption goods expressed in terms of date 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(0\leq\tau\leq t)$
\end_inset

, history 
\begin_inset Formula $s^{\tau}$
\end_inset

 consumption goods is not history dependent: 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}^{\tau}(s^{t})=q_{k}^{j}(\tilde{s}^{k})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j,k\geq0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $t-\tau=k-j$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[s_{\tau},s_{\tau+1},\ldots,s_{t}\right]=\left[\tilde{s}_{j},\tilde{s}_{j+1},\ldots,\tilde{s}_{k}\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using this proposition, we can verify that both the natural debt limits
 (21) and households' wealth levels (20) exhibit history independence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
A_{t}^{i}(s^{t}) & = & A^{i}(s_{t})\\
\Upsilon_{t}^{i}(s^{t}) & = & \Upsilon^{i}(s_{t})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

The finding concerning wealth levels (37) conveys a deep insight into how
 the sequential-trading competitive equilibrium attains the first-best outcome
 in which no idiosyncratic risk is borne by individual households.
 In particular, each household enters every period with a wealth level that
 is independent of past realizations of his endowment.
 That is, his past trades have fully insured him against the idiosyncratic
 outcomes of his endowment.
 And for that very same insurance motive, the household now enters the present
 period with a wealth level that is a function of the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 It is a state-contingent wealth level that was chosen by the household
 in the previous period 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

, and this wealth will be just sufficient to continue trading strategy designed
 to insure against future idiosyncractic risks.
 The optimal holding of wealth is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 only because the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

 determines the current endowment and contains all information that predicts
 future realizations of the household's endowment process (besides determing
 current prices and forecasts of future prices).
 It can be shown that a household tends to choose higher wealth levels for
 those states next period either that make his next period endowment low
 or more generally that signal poor future prospects for the household as
 compared to states that are more favorable to that particular household.
 Of course, these tendencies among individual households are modified by
 differences in the economy's aggregate endowment across states (as reflected
 in equilibrium asset prices).
 Aggregate shocks cannot be diversified away but must be borne by all of
 the households.
 The pricing kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(s_{t}|s_{t-1})$
\end_inset

 and the assumed clearing of all markets create the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

invisible hand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that coordinates households' transactions at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset

 in such a way that only aggregate risk and no idiosyncratic risk is borne
 by the households.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
8.9.3.
 Recursive formulation of optimizaiton and equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given that the pricing kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(s^{\prime}|s)$
\end_inset

 and the endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $y^{i}(s)$
\end_inset

 are functions of a Markov process 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

, we are motivated to seek a recursive solution to the household's optimization
 problem.
 Household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's state at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is its wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}^{i}$
\end_inset

 and the current realizaiton 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 We seek a pair of optimal policy functions 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{i}(a,s)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $g^{i}(a,s,s^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that the household's optimal decisions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
c_{t}^{i} & = & h^{i}(a_{t}^{i},s_{t})\\
a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1}) & = & g^{i}(a_{t}^{i},s_{t},s_{t+1})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{i}(a,s)$
\end_inset

 be the optimal value of household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's problem starting from state 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,s)$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{i}(a,s)$
\end_inset

 is the maximum expected discounted utility household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 with current wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 can attain in state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 The Bellman equation for the household's problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v^{i}(a,s)={\displaystyle \max_{c,\hat{a}(s^{\prime})}}\left\{ u(c)+\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\prime}}}v^{i}\left[\hat{a}(s^{\prime}),s^{\prime}\right]\pi(s^{\prime}|s)\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the maximization is subject to the following version of constraint
 (22)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c+{\displaystyle \sum_{s^{\prime}}}\hat{a}(s^{\prime})Q(s^{\prime}|s)\leq y^{i}(s)+a
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and also
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
c & \geq & 0\\
-\hat{a}(s^{\prime}) & \leq & A^{i}(s^{\prime}),\ \forall\ s^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Let the optimum decision rules be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
c & = & h^{i}(a,s)\\
\hat{a}(s^{\prime}) & = & g^{i}(a,s,s^{\prime})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Note that the solution of the Bellman equation implicitly depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(\cdot|\cdot)$
\end_inset

 because it appears in the constraint (41).
 In particular, use the first-order conditions for the problem on the right
 of equation (40) and the Benveniste-Scheinkman formula and rearrange to
 get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q(s_{t+1}|s_{t})=\beta\frac{u^{\prime}(c_{t+1}^{i})\pi(s_{t+1}|s_{t})}{u^{\prime}(c_{t}^{i})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where it is understood that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}^{i}=h^{i}(a_{t}^{i},s_{t})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+1}^{i}=h^{i}(a_{t+1}^{i}(s_{t+1}),s_{t+1})=h^{i}(g^{i}(a_{t}^{i},s_{t},s_{t+1}),s_{t+1})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
A 
\emph on
recursive competitive equilibrium
\emph default
 is an initial distribution of wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{a_{o}}$
\end_inset

, a pricing kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $Q(s^{\prime}|s)$
\end_inset

, sets of value functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ v^{i}(a,s)\right\} _{i=1}^{I}$
\end_inset

, and decision rules 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ h^{i}(a,s),g^{i}(a,s,s^{\prime})\right\} _{i=1}^{I}$
\end_inset

 such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
