\begin_layout Section*
Exercises
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Exercise 8.4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a pure endowment economy with a single representative consumer;
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ c_{t},d_{t}\right\} _{t=0}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 are the consumption and endowment processes, respectively.
 Feasible allocations satisfy
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{t}\leq d_{t}
\]

\end_inset

The endowment process is described by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{t+1}=\lambda_{t+1}d_{t}
\]

\end_inset

The growth rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t+1}$
\end_inset

 is described by a two-state Markov process with transition probabilities
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P_{ij}=\mbox{Prob}(\lambda_{t+1}=\bar{\lambda}_{j}|\lambda_{t}=\bar{\lambda}_{i})
\]

\end_inset

Assume that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
.8 & .2\\
.1 & .9
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

and that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{\lambda}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
.97\\
1.03
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}=.97$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{0}=1$
\end_inset

 are both known at date 0.
 The consumer has preferences over consumption ordered by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{0}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\frac{c_{t}^{1-\gamma}}{1-\gamma}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the mathematical expectation operator, conditioned on information known
 at time 0, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=2$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=.95$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part I
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At time 0, after 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}$
\end_inset

 are known, there are complete markets in date- and history-contingent claims.
 The market prices are denominated in units of time 0 consumption goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question B.
 
\series default
Compute the equilibrium price of a claim to one unit of consumption at date
 5, denominated in units of time 0 consumption, contingent on the following
 history of growth rates: 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\ldots,\lambda_{5})=(.97,.97,1.03,.97,1.03)$
\end_inset

.
 Please give a numerical answer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equilibrium price of a claim to one unit of consumption at date 5, denominat
ed in units of time 0 consumption, contigent on the given history of growth
 rates is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{5}^{0}(s^{5})=\frac{\beta^{5}u^{\prime}\left[c_{5}^{i}(s^{5})\right]\pi_{5}(s^{5})}{u^{\prime}\left[c_{0}^{i}(s^{0})\right]}
\]

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{5}=d_{5}=\lambda_{5}\lambda_{4}\lambda_{3}\lambda_{2}\lambda_{1}d_{0}=(0.97)^{3}(1.03)^{2}=.96825
\]

\end_inset

Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c)=c^{-\gamma}=c^{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c_{5})=u^{\prime}(.96825)=1.0666$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c_{0})=1$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi_{5}(s^{5}) & = & \pi(s_{5}|s_{4})\pi(s_{4}|s_{3})\pi(s_{3}|s_{2})\pi(s_{2}|s_{1})\pi(s_{1}|s_{0})\\
 & = & .8\times.8\times.2\times.1\times.2\\
 & = & .00256
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{5}^{0}(s^{5})=\frac{(0.95)^{5}(1.0666)(.00256)}{1}=.002113
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question C.
 
\series default
Compute the equilibrium price of a claim to one unit of consumption at date
 5, denominated in units of time 0 consumption, contingent on the following
 history of growth rates: 
\begin_inset Formula $(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\ldots,\lambda_{5})=(1.03,1.03,1.03,1.03,.97)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equilibrium price of a claim to one unit of consumption at date 5, denominat
ed in units of time 0 consumption, contigent on the given history of growth
 rates is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{5}^{0}(s^{5})=\frac{\beta^{5}u^{\prime}\left[c_{5}^{i}(s^{5})\right]\pi_{5}(s^{5})}{u^{\prime}\left[c_{0}^{i}(s^{0})\right]}
\]

\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{5}=d_{5}=\lambda_{5}\lambda_{4}\lambda_{3}\lambda_{2}\lambda_{1}d_{0}=(1.03)^{4}.97=1.09174
\]

\end_inset

Also 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c)=c^{-\gamma}=c^{-2}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c_{5})=u^{\prime}(1.09174)=0.839$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{\prime}(c_{0})=1$
\end_inset

.
 Finally,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\pi_{5}(s^{5}) & = & \pi(s_{5}|s_{4})\pi(s_{4}|s_{3})\pi(s_{3}|s_{2})\pi(s_{2}|s_{1})\pi(s_{1}|s_{0})\\
 & = & .2\times.9\times.9\times.9\times.1\\
 & = & 0.01458
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{5}^{0}(s^{5})=\frac{(0.95)^{2}(0.839)(0.01458)}{1}=0.009465
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Part II
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question I.
 
\series default
An entrepreneur enters this economy and proposes to issue a new security
 each period, namely, a risk-free two-period bond.
 Such a bond issued in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 promises to pay one unit of consumption at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 for sure.
 Find the price of this new security in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, contingent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the equivalence of allocations, the equation for the pricing kernel
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q(s_{t+1}|s^{t}) & = & \frac{q_{t+1}^{0}(s^{t+1})}{q_{t}^{0}(s^{t})}\\
 & = & \beta\frac{u^{\prime}\left[c_{t+1}^{i}(s^{t+1})\right]}{u^{\prime}\left[c_{t}^{i}(s^{t})\right]}\pi(s^{t+1}|s^{t})\\
 & = & \beta\pi(\lambda_{t+1}|\lambda_{t})\frac{c_{t}^{2}}{c_{t+1}^{2}}\\
 & = & \beta\pi(\lambda_{t+1}|\lambda_{t})\lambda_{t+1}^{-2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=d_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q(L|L) & = & \beta\cdot0.8\cdot(0.97)^{-2}\\
Q(H|L) & = & \beta\cdot0.2\cdot(1.03)^{-2}\\
Q(L|H) & = & \beta\cdot0.1\cdot(0.97)^{-2}\\
Q(H|H) & = & \beta\cdot0.9\cdot(1.03)^{-2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the price of the bond contingent on 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}=0.97$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(\lambda_{t}=L) & = & \beta\left[0.8\cdot(0.97)^{-2}+\beta\cdot0.2\cdot(1.03)^{-2}\right]\\
 & = & 0.9868
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This is the price a consumer has to pay in time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to get 1 unit of consumption for certain tomorrow.
 Finally, the real interest rate when 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}=0.97$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R(\lambda_{t}=L)=\left(\frac{1}{0.9868}-1\right)=1.3\%
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, there are two different real interest rates, one for
 when the shock is low, and one for when the shock is high.
 Hence, the real interest rates are fluctuating.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.2.
 Asset Euler equations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The agent has wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}>0$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and wants to use this wealth to maximize expected lifetime utility
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{t}\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}\beta^{j}u(c_{t+j})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes the mathematical expectation conditional on information known at
 time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a subjective discount factor, and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t+j}$
\end_inset

 is the agent's consumption in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+j$
\end_inset

.
 The utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is concave, strictly increasing, and twice continuously differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To finance future consumption, the agent can transfer wealth over time through
 bond and equity holdings.
 One-period bonds earn a risk-free real gross interest rate 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}$
\end_inset

, measured in untis of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 consumption good per time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 consumption good.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 be gross payout on the agent's bond holdings between period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, payable in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 with a present value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{t}^{-1}L_{t}$
\end_inset

 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The variable 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 is negative if the agent issues bonds and thereby borrows funds.
 The agent's holdings of equity shares between periods 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

 are denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

, where a negative number indicates a short position in shares.
 A share of a equity entitles the owner to its stochastic dividend stream
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 be the share price in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 net of that period's dividend.
 The budget constraint becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{t}+R_{t}^{-1}L_{t}+p_{t}N_{t}\leq A_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the next period's wealth is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
A_{t+1}=L_{t}+(p_{t+1}+y_{t+1})N_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The stochasitc dividend is the only source of exogenous fundamental uncertainty,
 with properties to be specified as needed later.
 The agent's maximization problem is then a dynamic programming problem
 with the state at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 being 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 and current and past 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, and the contols being 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 At interior solutions, the Euler equations associated with controls 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
u^{\prime}(c_{t})R_{t}^{-1} & = & E_{t}\beta u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})\\
u^{\prime}(c_{t})p_{t} & = & E_{t}\beta(y_{t+1}+p_{t+1})u^{\prime}(c_{t+1})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

These Euler equations give a number of insights into asset prices and consumptio
n.
 Before turning to these we first note that an optimal solution to the agent's
 maximizaiton problem must also satisfy the following transversality conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}E_{t}\beta^{k}u^{\prime}(c_{t+k})R_{t+k}^{-1}L_{t+k} & = & 0\\
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}E_{t}\beta^{k}u^{\prime}(c_{t+k})p_{t+k}N_{t+k} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heuristically, if any of the expressions in equations (52) and (53) were
 strictly positive, the agent would be overaccumulating assets so that a
 higher expected lifetime utility could be achieved by, for example, increasing
 consumption today.
 The counterpart to such nonoptimality in a finite horizon model would be
 that the agent dies with positive asset holdings.
 For reasons like thos in a ifnite horizon model, the agent would be happy
 if the two conditions (52) and (53) could be violated on the negative side.
 But the market would stop the agent from financing consumption by accumulating
 the debts that would be associated with such violations of (52) and (53).
 No other agent would want to make those loans.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.5.
 Equilibrium asset pricing
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now explore equilibrium asset pricing in a simple representative agent
 endowment economy, Lucas's asset-pricing model.
 We imagine an economy consisting of a large number of identical agents
 with preferences as specified in expression (47).
 The only durable good in the economy is a set of identical 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

trees,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on for each person in the economy.
 At the beginning of each period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, each tree yields dividends in amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The fruit, or dividends, are not storable, but the tree is perfectly durable.
 Each agent starts life at time zero with one tree.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The dividend 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be governed by a Markov process, and it is a function of
 the sole state variable of the economy, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}=y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 The time-invariant transition probability distribution function is given
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Prob}(s_{t+1}\leq s^{\prime}|s_{t}=s)=F(s^{\prime},s)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

All agents maximize expression (47) subject to the budget constraint (48)-(49)
 and transversality conditions (52)-(53).
 In an equilibrium, asset prices clear the markets.
 That is, the bond holdings of all agents sum to zero, and their total stock
 positions are equal to the aggregate number of shares.
 As a normalization, let there be one share per tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Due to the assumption that all agents are identical with respect to both
 preferences and endowments, we can work with a representative agent.
 Lucas's model shares features with a variety of representative agent asset-pric
ing models.
 These use versions of stochastic optimal growth models to generate allocations
 and price assets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Such asset pricing models can be constructed by the following steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Describe the preferences, technology, and endowments of a dynamic economy,
 then solve for the equilibrium intertemporal consumption allocation.
 Sometimes there is a particular planning problem whose solution equals
 the competitive allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Set up a competitive market in some particular asset that represents a
 specific claim on future consumption goods.
 Permit agents to buy and sell at equilibrium asset prices subject to particular
 borrowing and short-sales constriants.
 Find an agent's Euler equation for this asset.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Equate the consumption that appears in the Euler equation derived in step
 2 to the equilibrium consumption in step 1.
 This procedure will give the asset price at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as a function of the state of the economy at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In our endowment economy, a planner that treats all agents the same would
 like to maximize 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{0}\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}u(c_{t})$
\end_inset

 subject to 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}\leq y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Evidently the solution is to set 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}$
\end_inset

 equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 After substituting this consumption allocation into equations (50) and
 (51), we arrive at expressions for the risk-free interest rate and the
 share price:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
u^{\prime}(y_{t})R_{t}^{-1} & = & E_{t}\beta u^{\prime}(y_{t+1})\\
u^{\prime}(y_{t})p_{t} & = & E_{t}\beta(y_{t+1}+p_{t+1})u^{\prime}(y_{t+1})
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.6.
 Stock prices without bubbles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using recursions on equation (55) and the law of iterated expectations,
 which states that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}E_{t+1}(\cdot)=E_{t}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, we arrive at the following expression for the equilibrium share price:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u^{\prime}(y_{t})p_{t}=E_{t}\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\beta^{j}u^{\prime}(y_{t+j})y_{t+j}+E_{t}\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\beta^{k}u^{\prime}(y_{t+k})p_{t+k}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Moreover, equilibrium share prices have to be consistent with market clearing;
 that is, agents must be willing to hold their endowments of trees forever.
 It follows immediately that the last term in the equation must be zero.
 We can therefore conclude that the equilibrium price must satisfy
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
p_{t} & = & E_{t}\left[\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\beta^{j}\frac{u^{\prime}(y_{t+j})}{u^{\prime}(y_{t})}y_{t+j}\right]\\
 & = & E_{t}\left[\sum_{j=t+1}^{\infty}\beta^{j-t}\frac{u^{\prime}(c_{j})}{u^{\prime}(c_{t})}y_{j}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \sum_{j=t+1}^{\infty}\sum_{s_{j}}\frac{\beta^{j}\pi(s_{j})u^{\prime}(c(s_{j}))}{\beta^{t}\pi(s_{t})u^{\prime}(c(s_{t}))}y(s_{j})\nonumber \\
* & = & \sum_{j=t+1}^{\infty}\sum_{s_{j}}\frac{q_{j}^{0}(s_{j})}{q_{t}^{0}(s_{t})}\nonumber \\
* & = & \sum_{j=t+1}^{\infty}\sum_{s_{j}}q_{j}^{t}(s_{j})\nonumber \\
* & = & \sum_{j=t+1}^{\infty}\sum_{s_{j}}Q_{j}(s_{j}|s_{t})\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{j}(s_{j}|s_{t})$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

-step pricing kernel which gives the price of one unit of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 periods ahead, contingent on the state in that future period being 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j}$
\end_inset

, given that the current state is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this model, good 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 has become the numeraire.
 Moreover, under the asusmption that the endowments are governed by a Markov
 process, the representative household's endowment in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is a time invariant measurable function of 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}(s^{t})=y_{t}(s_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Since it has been shown that each individual's consumption is a function
 only of the current realization of the aggregate endowment, then it follows
 immediately that the equilibrium allocation is a function only of the current
 state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}(s^{t})=c(s_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Also, the pricing kernel is a function only of the current state, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{j}(s_{j}|s^{t})=Q(s_{j}|s_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, it follows that the equilibrium price can be expressed as a function
 of the current state 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{t}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}=p(s_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we have established the history independence of all the variables
 in this model economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
13.7.
 Computing asset prices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now turn to three examples in which it is easy to calculate an asset-pricing
 funciton by solving the expectational difference equation (55).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
13.7.1.
 Example 1: logarithmic preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Take the special case of equation (57) that emerges when 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c_{t})=\ln(c_{t})$
\end_inset

 then equation (57) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{t}=\frac{\beta}{1-\beta}y_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Equation (58) is our asset-pricing funciton.
 It maps the state of the economy at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

, into the price of a lucas tree at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
13.7.2.
 Example 2: a finite-state version
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a discrete version of Lucas's one-kind-of-tree model.
 Let dividends assume the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 possible distinct values 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\ldots,\sigma_{n}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Let dividends evolve through time according to a Markov chain, with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Prob}\{y_{t+1}=\sigma_{l}|y_{t}=\sigma_{k}\}=P_{kl}>0
\]

\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $(n\times n)$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 with element 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{kl}$
\end_inset

 is called a stochastic matrix.
 The matrix satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{l=1}^{n}P_{kl}=1$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 Express equation (55) of Lucas's model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{t}u^{\prime}(y_{t})=\beta E_{t}p_{t+1}u^{\prime}(y_{t+1})+\beta E_{t}y_{t+1}u^{\prime}(y_{t+1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Express the price at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 as a function of the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}=p(\sigma_{k})$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}u^{\prime}(y_{t})=p(\sigma_{k})u^{\prime}(\sigma_{k})\equiv v_{k},\ k=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
 Also, define 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{k}=\beta E_{t}y_{t+1}u^{\prime}(y_{t+1})=\beta\sum_{l=1}^{n}\sigma_{l}u^{\prime}(\sigma_{l})P_{kl}$
\end_inset

.
 Then equation (59) can be expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\sigma_{k})u^{\prime}(\sigma_{k})=\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{n}}p(\sigma_{l})u^{\prime}(\sigma_{l})P_{kl}+\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{n}}\sigma_{l}u^{\prime}(\sigma_{l})P_{kl}
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{k}=\alpha_{k}+\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{n}}P_{kl}v_{l}
\]

\end_inset

or in matrix terms, 
\begin_inset Formula $v=\alpha+\beta Pv$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 are column vectors.
 The equation can be represented as 
\begin_inset Formula $(I-\beta P)v=\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 This equation has a unique solution given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
v=(1-\beta P)^{-1}\alpha
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The price of the asset in state 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

, call it 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}$
\end_inset

, can then be found from 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}=\nicefrac{v_{k}}{\left[u^{\prime}(\sigma_{k})\right]}$
\end_inset

.
 Notice that equation (6) can be represented as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v=(I+\beta P+\beta^{2}P^{2}+\ldots)\alpha
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(\sigma_{k})=p_{k}={\displaystyle \sum_{l}}\left(I+\beta P+\beta^{2}P^{2}+\ldots\right)_{kl}\frac{\alpha_{l}}{u^{\prime}(\sigma_{k})}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(I+\beta P+\beta^{2}P^{2}+\ldots\right)_{kl}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $(k,l)$
\end_inset

 element of the matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(I+\beta P+\beta^{2}P^{2}+\ldots\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
13.7.3.
 Example 3: asset pricing with growth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let's price a Lucas tree in a pure endowment economy with 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{t}=y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t+1}=\lambda_{t+1}y_{t}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 is Markob with transition matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 be the ex-dividend price of the Lucas tree.
 Assume the CRRA utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u(c)=\nicefrac{c^{1-\gamma}}{(1-\gamma)}$
\end_inset

.
 Evidently, the price of the Lucas tree satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{t}=E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\frac{c_{t+1}}{c_{t}}\right)^{-\gamma}\left(p_{t+1}+y_{t+1}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Dividing both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and rearranging gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{p_{t}}{y_{t}}=E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\lambda_{t+1}\right)^{1-\gamma}\left(\frac{p_{t+1}}{y_{t+1}}+1\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{i}=\beta{\displaystyle \sum_{j}}P_{ij}\lambda_{j}^{1-\gamma}(w_{j}+1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the price-dividend ratio.
\end_layout

